There are several potential explanations why black individuals may be represented more commonly in relation to certain forms of criminal activity like robbery: 1) Historically, many individuals from pre-civil war times through present times had to pursue economic means beyond traditional legal labor to provide themselves and their families with income (either legal or extralegal labor) due to limited employment opportunities;  
2) Many studies have linked poverty and inequality in the black community to higher levels of stress, which can lead individuals to seek quick results from antisocial outlets; 
3) Many jurisdictions place a heavy emphasis on apprehension and incarceration versus job training for black offenders (resulting in fewer job/skills opportunities); 4) Black cultural values and norms may promote risk-taking in pursuit of quick money and respect amongst peers; and  5) Many robberies appear to be organized, meaning black offenders have access to networks providing training, supplies, contacts, etc. Thus, providing structure and protection. These five elements provide a foundation for understanding robbery as it relates to the black community, but other individual motivations and variations come into play (e.g., peer pressure).
